# Hard Constraints

## 1) Commit preparation gate

- "Prepare commit" means draft message and readiness checks, not immediate commit.
- Never run `git commit` or push without explicit user instruction to execute.
- Draft from actual diffs, not from guessed intent.

## 2) Planning source discipline

- Use trusted planning sources first: `docs/ai/ACTIVE_WORK.md`, `FEATURE_REGISTRY.md`, `TECH_DEBT.md`, recent `PROGRESS_LOG.md`, code/tests.
- Do not infer backlog from snapshot/archive/reference docs.

## 3) Refactor discipline

- Refactor work should target a clear end state.
- Avoid leaving silent dual-path temporary layers; either remove or register debt.

## 4) Context recovery

When context may be stale, re-bootstrap from:
- `docs/ai/WORKFLOW_PROFILE.md`
- `docs/ai/BOOTSTRAP_DIGEST.md`
- `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`
- `docs/ai/ACTIVE_WORK.md`

## 5) Comment hygiene (IDs stay out of code comments)

- **Forbidden** in source comments / code-adjacent inline docs: Feature, Slice, Bug, or ADR IDs (e.g. `CORE-F01`, `CORE-F01-S02`, `BUG-CORE-001`, `ADR-20261015-01`).
- IDs belong in collaboration docs (registry, ACTIVE_WORK, design/plan/progress) and may appear in commit messages when the team uses them there.
- Comments explain non-obvious *why*, invariants, hazards, or trade-offs; keep them short; do not restate the next line of code.

## 6) Branch naming (topic granularity; no registry IDs)

- Feature branches use topic paths: `feat/<topic>` or `feat/<topic>/<subtopic>` (e.g. `feat/render`, `feat/render/material`, `feat/gameplay`).
- Name the **area of change**, not a Feature/Slice row. Prefer an existing matching area branch over a new ID-tied branch.
- **Forbidden:** branch names built around registry IDs (e.g. `feat/core-f01-xxx`, `feat/TMPL-F05`, `feat/core-f01-s01`).
- Same ID ban applies to `fix/` / `refactor/` / `chore/` (and similar): topic words only.
- Profile cannot disable §5 or §6.
